former New Orleans Mayor Mitch Landrieu  helped lead the charge to remove  Confederate statues in his city now he's  leading an initiative that calls for a  reckoning with the legacy of racism the  nonprofit a pluribus unum works to  achieve social and racial justice in the  u.s. the efforts name is taken of course  from the u.s. motto that means out of  many one its first report divided by  design interesting title offers a  comprehensive look at race and class  issues in the South Landrieu and his  team talked to more than 800 people from  28 communities in 13 different states he  joins us at the table to unpack what he  found out how did you decide where you  were going and who you were going to  talk to specific I'm gonna answer that  question but I'm stunned I went to Peter  lubbers when we traveled across the  south it was terrific we went to 28  different counties we went to 13  different states so we criss cross we  were people that you talk to like talk  to everybody we took well everybody that  that that is african-american white you  know educated non-college educated I  mean we we wanted to get on the ground  and talk to and listen to people we  conducted over 800 interviews some of  them lasted longer than a half an hour  45 minutes where we talked to individual  people so that we can listen in here and  get out of this noise that you hear all  the time from Washington DC and get back  on the ground so we came up with /  design what does that mean yeah well it  what it means as it is that there is a  big difference the way people see the  world everybody wants equal opportunity  for themselves they want the kids to  have a better life but the  african-american community views the  obstacles there differently from the  white community yeah white folks that  responded to the survey think that  racism is just an individual act of  malice to another person that looks  different from them african-americans  say that's not our experience while they  accept that as a form of racism because  they feel it every day  they say the institutions that we have  are still baked with institutional  designs and so the point is the country  is the way it is because we designed it  this way right and so if we want a  different outcome we have to redesign it  so criminal justice reform for example  would run into this education reform how  you gerrymander legislative districts  that are etc but Mitch how do you  advance this conversation because to get  to those  you talk about you have to you have to  get support from the white community to  do it yeah there's no question and the  first thing that has to happen is people  need to get to know each other spend  time with each other see each other's  humanity and you have to do this on the  ground you have to do with family in the  family neighborhood the neighborhood  church the church team - team and  community to community you heard many  white people say listen this happened  how can you hold us accountable for  something that happened - 150 years and  when it comes to reparations what would  you the way that we talk about this is  listen it may not be your fault yeah but  it is certainly your responsibility so  in the last segment you had climate  change you know what the impact is going  to be in a couple years demographic  changes if we don't get together across  race in class the chances of us dealing  with those complicated issues that are  going to stress us it's going to make  the country worse and finally the reason  we use a pluribus unum is because of  founding fathers from our origin set out  of many we are one now the good thing  about the poll is that everybody thinks  that diversity is a strength not a  weakness and of course that issue is  being challenged on the national level  yeah on the ground in America most  people in the south that responded to  this survey liked the fact that they  live around it but we're kind of  segregated in isolation right now and we  have to work our way out of that if we  want to be strong and be the country  that we all say we aspire to be many of  these issues come up in the democratic  primary race for president here and I  know you said you're not gonna run in  2020 but I'm curious if someone asked  you to be vice president would you be  interested  [Laughter]  you've been you opted not as we  mentioned you you're not running you've  been very supportive of former Vice  President Joe Biden are you still a fan  well I haven't endorsed anybody yet but  people have asked me and I think he is  our best chance to do what I think the  country really wants to do which is turn  a corner and have a new president so  that we can reclaim what America really  is supposed to be I think you listen I  think people that are worried about it  or more worried I think he's doing fine  think about it he's been out there he's  gotten everything but the kitchen sink  thrown at him from everybody he still  stand if he's still leading and he still  is going well it's a long campaign there  are lots of candidates from my  perspective any one of them would be  exponentially better than what we have  right now and given what you're seeing  in Washington today the country wants to  make a change and I think they will what  do you think about the lieutenant  colonel who  defied yesterday highly credentialed and  now he's being attacked to someone I  think that not patriotic here's the  thing at some point in time the Senators  need to put that country above the party  and the information that's coming out  from people who are Republicans that  were appointed by the president that  served this country honorably and  actually will wound it who themselves a  very credible people should be given  great weight impeachment is a very  sobering thing it should be handled that  way and the Congress should do their  duty the American people are smart  enough to figure this out over time the  other impeachment process that Nixon  went through took a long long period of  time so I think walking through this  very soberly but to attack somebody's  credibility like here the ambassador  testified they're very credible  witnesses and their stories ring true  from anybody that's ever served on a  jury and I've served two it sounds  pretty credible that they were able to  see and hear and know what occurred and  then the stories make sense so let the  evidence fall where it made but don't  don't don't malign this man's character  who bled fraud card there was one thing  from your report that I thought was  interesting you said talent is equally  distributed but opportunity isn't that  did not come from you that came from  high school students in Louisville  Kentucky looked at me and said mr. Mehra  well you know we're out here we're  working hard we're all good we're smart  but we can't get ahead you know talent  out here is equally distributed but our  opportunities are not and if the country  could just stay focused on that so that  everybody has equal opportunity and  everybody has a fair shot we've got a  chance as a country to get where we say  we want to thank you for nudging our  focus and that yeah sure Landrieu great  decision
 

